{
  "themes": [
    {
      "machine_theme_id": "M1",
      "label": "Implicit link between meat eating and masculinity",
      "one_sentence_description": "Men may view eating meat, particularly red meat, as a default and normal choice that is implicitly linked to masculinity, even if they do not consciously think about it.",
      "relevance": "central",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "turn_id": "T002",
          "speaker": "Participant 1",
          "quote": "when you're deciding what to eat, there's definitely this thing where meat just feels like the normal choice, you know? And I think part of that is just because that's what you grow up with, what's around you. But there's also probably a bit of that masculinity thing baked in there somewhere, even if you're not really thinking about it directly."
        },
        {
          "turn_id": "T003",
          "speaker": "Participant 2",
          "quote": "Yeah, I think Isaiah's onto something there actually. Like, I wouldn't say I sit around thinking about it either, but... meat just feels normal, doesn't it?"
        }
      ],
      "voiced_by": [
        "Participant 1",
        "Participant 2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "machine_theme_id": "M2",
      "label": "Vegetarianism as a gendered statement carrying social assumptions",
      "one_sentence_description": "Choosing to become vegetarian is perceived as a significant decision that carries social assumptions about a man's identity and masculinity, going beyond a simple difference in food preference.",
      "relevance": "central",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "turn_id": "T005",
          "speaker": "Participant 2",
          "quote": "Because if being vegetarian or whatever — if that wasn't tied to something people think about masculinity or what a bloke should be, then it wouldn't carry that extra thing with it."
        },
        {
          "turn_id": "T004",
          "speaker": "Participant 1",
          "quote": "Like, there's noticing something's different, and then there's noticing it and it meaning something about you as a bloke."
        }
      ],
      "voiced_by": [
        "Participant 2",
        "Participant 1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "machine_theme_id": "M3",
      "label": "Lack of performative masculinity in close-knit rural environments",
      "one_sentence_description": "In rural, tight-knit communities where individuals are well-known to each other, dietary choices are not interpreted as a performative statement about one's masculinity.",
      "relevance": "central",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "turn_id": "T007",
          "speaker": "Participant 3",
          "quote": "But when you've known someone since primary school, when you've been eating meat because that's just what your family does, nobody's really reading it as this statement about your identity or whatever. It's just... what you do."
        }
      ],
      "voiced_by": [
        "Participant 3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "machine_theme_id": "M4",
      "label": "Meat eating as a performative safe choice in urban peer groups",
      "one_sentence_description": "In urban settings with greater anonymity, eating meat can serve as a performative, safe choice that avoids inviting questions or negative social assumptions from peers.",
      "relevance": "central",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "turn_id": "T008",
          "speaker": "Participant 2",
          "quote": "I'm choosing to eat meat partly because it's normal and habit, but also partly because it's the safe choice, you know? The one that doesn't invite questions or assumptions about me."
        }
      ],
      "voiced_by": [
        "Participant 2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "machine_theme_id": "M5",
      "label": "Group conformity rather than gender as the driver of dietary social norms",
      "one_sentence_description": "Deviating from common dietary choices is primarily about sticking out in one's social circle rather than expressing or challenging gender roles.",
      "relevance": "secondary",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "turn_id": "T004",
          "speaker": "Participant 1",
          "quote": "So maybe it's less about gender and more just... what's normal in your circle. You stick out if you're doing something different, whatever that is."
        }
      ],
      "voiced_by": [
        "Participant 1"
      ]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "themes": [
    {
      "machine_theme_id": "M1",
      "label": "Gender does not influence food choices",
      "one_sentence_description": "Participants agree that gender plays no role in what they choose to eat, rejecting the notion of masculine or feminine foods in favor of personal taste.",
      "relevance": "central",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "turn_id": "T002",
          "speaker": "Participant 1",
          "quote": "I don't think gender influences what I eat at all."
        },
        {
          "turn_id": "T005",
          "speaker": "Participant 4",
          "quote": "I agree that gender doesn't play any role whatsoever"
        },
        {
          "turn_id": "T006",
          "speaker": "Participant 5",
          "quote": "Yeah, same with me. I've never even thought about it until I read that question, to be honest."
        }
      ],
      "voiced_by": [
        "Participant 1",
        "Participant 2",
        "Participant 3",
        "Participant 4",
        "Participant 5"
      ]
    },
    {
      "machine_theme_id": "M2",
      "label": "Portion size rather than food type may differ by gender",
      "one_sentence_description": "Some participants note that gender differences might affect the portion size or quantity of food eaten rather than the type of food itself.",
      "relevance": "secondary",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "turn_id": "T003",
          "speaker": "Participant 2",
          "quote": "maybe I can eat more."
        },
        {
          "turn_id": "T004",
          "speaker": "Participant 3",
          "quote": "maybe about portions, but nothing in particular."
        }
      ],
      "voiced_by": [
        "Participant 2",
        "Participant 3"
      ]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "themes": [
    {
      "machine_theme_id": "M1",
      "label": "Avoiding the perception of caring too much about food as a man",
      "one_sentence_description": "Men face social pressure to avoid appearing overly interested, fussy, or precious about food, as showing too much care or pickiness is seen as unmasculine or trying too hard.",
      "relevance": "central",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "turn_id": "T002",
          "speaker": "Participant 1",
          "quote": "if you're getting really into cooking or you're fussy about where your stuff comes from or whatever, that's sometimes seen as a bit... soft? Or like you're trying too hard. It's more of a bloke thing to just eat what's put in front of you, not make a fuss about it."
        },
        {
          "turn_id": "T004",
          "speaker": "Participant 2",
          "quote": "if you're *too* into it — like if you're the guy who's always going on about where the ingredients are from or you're being picky about it — that's still kind of... I dunno, it reads a bit different. Not weak exactly, but like, a bit try-hard maybe?"
        }
      ],
      "voiced_by": [
        "Participant 1",
        "Participant 2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "machine_theme_id": "M2",
      "label": "Self-policing and internalized shame limit men's food choices",
      "one_sentence_description": "Men internalize social expectations about food, leading them to self-police their choices and stick with familiar routines rather than risk judgment or shame.",
      "relevance": "central",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "turn_id": "T005",
          "speaker": "Participant 1",
          "quote": "that's probably more of what's keeping me in the same routine than just the lack of options or the effort. It's more about not wanting to stand out as the bloke who's too bothered about his food."
        },
        {
          "turn_id": "T008",
          "speaker": "Participant 2",
          "quote": "It's shame, almost. Like, there's this feeling that it's not normal, it's not what blokes do, and you just... accept that without questioning it much."
        }
      ],
      "voiced_by": [
        "Participant 1",
        "Participant 2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "machine_theme_id": "M3",
      "label": "Amplified social pressure and policing in rural environments",
      "one_sentence_description": "In close-knit rural communities, the lack of separate social circles and high visibility make self-policing even stronger, as any deviation from food norms quickly spreads to family and neighbors.",
      "relevance": "secondary",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "turn_id": "T007",
          "speaker": "Participant 1",
          "quote": "you end up policing yourself harder because the potential reach of it is bigger. It's easier to just fit in and not stand out, because standing out is actually quite noticeable when everyone's in the same place all the time."
        }
      ],
      "voiced_by": [
        "Participant 1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "machine_theme_id": "M4",
      "label": "Gendered associations of specific foods",
      "one_sentence_description": "Certain foods, such as salads or aesthetically refined dishes, carry feminine or 'soft' connotations, causing men to feel a persistent hesitation when ordering them.",
      "relevance": "central",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "turn_id": "T010",
          "speaker": "Participant 2",
          "quote": "Like, salads. You don't really see your mates getting a big salad for lunch, do you? It's just... that's a girl thing, or it comes across that way."
        },
        {
          "turn_id": "T012",
          "speaker": "Participant 3",
          "quote": "I'll be looking at a menu and there's something that looks decent but it's got salad on the side or it's a bit... I dunno, refined or whatever, and there's this immediate thought of like, nah, that's not really a bloke order, is it?"
        }
      ],
      "voiced_by": [
        "Participant 2",
        "Participant 3"
      ]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "themes": [
    {
      "machine_theme_id": "M1",
      "label": "Social judgment and meat-free men",
      "one_sentence_description": "Men face social judgment, comments, and teasing when they choose not to eat meat because meat-eating is tied to masculinity.",
      "relevance": "central",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "turn_id": "T002",
          "speaker": "Participant 1",
          "quote": "And then if you're a bloke choosing not to, there's this whole other thing that gets attached to it, isn't there?"
        },
        {
          "turn_id": "T004",
          "speaker": "Participant 2",
          "quote": "bloke says he's vegetarian or he doesn't eat red meat, and there's this... I don't know, this immediate thing that happens. Someone'll make a comment, or there's this bit of piss-taking that goes beyond just banter, you know?"
        }
      ],
      "voiced_by": [
        "Participant 1",
        "Participant 2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "machine_theme_id": "M2",
      "label": "Meat-eating as the unquestioned default for men",
      "one_sentence_description": "Eating meat is the unquestioned default choice for men, meaning they are never expected to explain or defend their food choices.",
      "relevance": "central",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "turn_id": "T002",
          "speaker": "Participant 1",
          "quote": "But I'm aware that's probably because nobody's ever made me feel like I should justify it or feel awkward about it."
        },
        {
          "turn_id": "T004",
          "speaker": "Participant 2",
          "quote": "Whereas for me, eating meat — nobody cares. Nobody needs to know why, it's just what I do. It's the default, so it doesn't need justifying."
        }
      ],
      "voiced_by": [
        "Participant 1",
        "Participant 2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "machine_theme_id": "M3",
      "label": "Food-related judgment as group enforcement and insecurity",
      "one_sentence_description": "The judgment and pushback received by men who do not eat meat is driven by group dynamics, insecurity, and the desire to enforce conformity rather than the food itself.",
      "relevance": "central",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "turn_id": "T004",
          "speaker": "Participant 2",
          "quote": "it's not actually about the food, it's about this weird masculine thing that's got attached to it, and I think that's... I mean, that's just silly, isn't it?"
        },
        {
          "turn_id": "T005",
          "speaker": "Participant 1",
          "quote": "it's more about the group, I reckon. It's like, \"we do this, that's who we are, and if you're not doing it you're saying something about us.\""
        }
      ],
      "voiced_by": [
        "Participant 1",
        "Participant 2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "machine_theme_id": "M4",
      "label": "Indifference diffuses food teasing",
      "one_sentence_description": "Teasing and peer pressure over food choices fade away if the individual remains unbothered and refuses to act defensively.",
      "relevance": "secondary",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "turn_id": "T005",
          "speaker": "Participant 1",
          "quote": "And over time the piss-taking just... died away, because he wasn't bothered by it. He wasn't defending himself or getting wound up about it."
        },
        {
          "turn_id": "T006",
          "speaker": "Participant 2",
          "quote": "The piss-taking only works if it lands, doesn't it?"
        }
      ],
      "voiced_by": [
        "Participant 1",
        "Participant 2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "machine_theme_id": "M5",
      "label": "Maturity reduces compliance with food stereotypes",
      "one_sentence_description": "As men grow older and more mature, they care less about conforming to group expectations around food and are less affected by judgment.",
      "relevance": "secondary",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "turn_id": "T005",
          "speaker": "Participant 1",
          "quote": "I'm old enough not to care about that sort of thing."
        },
        {
          "turn_id": "T006",
          "speaker": "Participant 2",
          "quote": "once you're past that — once you're forty, you've got your own life sorted, you don't need to prove anything — you just don't care."
        }
      ],
      "voiced_by": [
        "Participant 1",
        "Participant 2"
      ]
    }
  ]
}

{"themes":[{"machine_theme_id":"M1","label":"Retention of Choice Amidst Limited Local Availability","one_sentence_description":"Even when geographic or retail constraints limit the types of food available, individuals still retain the agency to make deliberate choices about what they eat.","relevance":"secondary","evidence":[{"turn_id":"T002","speaker":"Participant 1","quote":"Well, out here it's a bit different, isn't it? I mean, you're limited by what's actually available. I can't just decide I fancy something and pop down the road to get it. The Co-op's got what it's got, and that's largely it. So a lot of the time you eat what you can get, what's in stock."},{"turn_id":"T002","speaker":"Participant 1","quote":"You've got less options, that's a fact. But you still choose what you do with what you've got."}],"voiced_by":["Participant 1"]},{"machine_theme_id":"M2","label":"Gender and Generational Conditioning of Dietary Habits","one_sentence_description":"Dietary preferences are strongly shaped by generational and traditional expectations of what is appropriate for men to eat, resulting in a preference for simple, substantial food over fussier options.","relevance":"central","evidence":[{"turn_id":"T004","speaker":"Participant 2","quote":"Look, at my age, in my generation, you grow up with certain ideas about what men eat and what they don't, don't you? You're not sitting around worrying too much about what you're having — you just want something that fills you up, something substantial."},{"turn_id":"T006","speaker":"Participant 3","quote":"You had certain ideas about what was men's food and what wasn't. Meat and potatoes, that sort of thing. And you didn't really think about it, you just... that was what you ate."},{"turn_id":"T012","speaker":"Participant 4","quote":"I grew up the same way Patrick did — meat and potatoes, that's what you ate, that's what men did. And I've stuck with that, largely."}],"voiced_by":["Participant 2","Participant 3","Participant 4"]},{"machine_theme_id":"M3","label":"Loosening Dietary Constraints and Adapting Food Habits with Age","one_sentence_description":"With age and changing health requirements, traditional ideas about masculine eating habits can loosen, enabling men to change their diet for their well-being without losing their personal identity.","relevance":"central","evidence":[{"turn_id":"T006","speaker":"Participant 3","quote":"I eat a lot more veg now than I used to. More fish. My wife got me into it, partly, but also I just... I've noticed it makes me feel better. Feel less sluggish. And I stopped caring quite as much about whether it looked \"manly\" or whatever."},{"turn_id":"T008","speaker":"Participant 3","quote":"You don't have to become someone else to eat differently, you know? You can still be you. It's just... maybe it's not as fixed as it feels from the inside. Things can shift without you having to abandon something that matters."},{"turn_id":"T010","speaker":"Participant 1","quote":"I know who I am, and that's solid enough that trying something different won't shake it."}],"voiced_by":["Participant 1","Participant 3"]},{"machine_theme_id":"M4","label":"Resisting Change Due to Food Habits Being Tied to Personal Identity","one_sentence_description":"Long-standing eating habits can become deeply fused with self-identity, causing any pressure or attempt to change them to feel like a threat to who one is.","relevance":"central","evidence":[{"turn_id":"T007","speaker":"Participant 2","quote":"But I've been eating the same way for over seventy years now. It's not just a habit, is it? It's... it's who I am, sort of. The way I think about food, about what's proper to eat. And I know that sounds daft when you put it like that, but it's different when it's actually you living it."},{"turn_id":"T007","speaker":"Participant 2","quote":"I look at myself and I think, I'm not going to change that now. I don't want to. It feels... I don't know, it feels like giving up something that matters, even if I can't quite explain why."}],"voiced_by":["Participant 2"]},{"machine_theme_id":"M5","label":"Lack of Motivation to Alter Diet When Satisfied with Current Habits","one_sentence_description":"There is little incentive or desire to change one's diet if one feels healthy and content with existing food choices, regardless of how traditional or unvaried they are.","relevance":"secondary","evidence":[{"turn_id":"T012","speaker":"Participant 4","quote":"But I've not woken up sluggish and thought, right, I need to change this. I feel alright as I am. So when you say the door's not locked, Fletcher, and Keith says it might surprise you — I get that. But for me it's not really about whether I could change. It's that I don't have a reason to. I'm not unhappy with how things are."}],"voiced_by":["Participant 4"]
}
]}
